---
title: "Platform resources"
description: "Buy phone numbers, connect email domains and Telegram bots, and configure voice calling."
---

`photon project platform` manages the resources a project sends and receives on
— SMS numbers, iMessage lines, email domains, Telegram bots, WhatsApp Business
numbers, and voice routing.

```text
photon project platform
├── list
├── operation
│   └── cancel <operation-id> [--yes]
├── email
│   ├── list [--limit 30] [--page-token <token>]
│   ├── connect <domain> [--idempotency-key <key>] [--no-wait] [--no-browser]
│   ├── dns <operation-id>
│   └── disconnect <resource-id> [--yes]
├── sms
│   ├── list [--limit 30] [--page-token <token>]
│   ├── purchase [--country US] [--area-code <code>]
│   │            [--idempotency-key <key>] [--yes]
│   └── delete <resource-id> [--yes]
├── telegram
│   ├── list [--limit 30] [--page-token <token>]
│   ├── connect [--idempotency-key <key>] [--no-wait] [--no-browser]
│   ├── view <resource-id>
│   └── disconnect <resource-id> [--yes]
├── imessage
│   ├── view
│   ├── list [--limit 30] [--page-token <token>]
│   ├── purchase [--idempotency-key <key>] [--yes]
│   └── assignment
│       ├── list [--include-released] [--limit 30] [--page-token]
│       ├── create --phone-number <E.164> [--email]
│       │          [--first-name] [--last-name] [--idempotency-key <key>]
│       ├── view <assignment-id>
│       └── release <assignment-id> [--yes]
├── whatsapp-business
│   ├── account
│   │   └── list
│   └── number
│       ├── list [--limit 30] [--page-token <token>]
│       ├── view <resource-id>
│       ├── add [--source <byon|photon>]
│       │       [--waba-id <id> | --new-waba]
│       │       [--voip-resource-id <id>] [--display-name <name>]
│       │       [--idempotency-key <key>] [--no-browser]
│       └── delete <resource-id> [--yes]
└── voice
    ├── list [--limit 30] [--page-token <token>]
    ├── profile
    │   ├── list [--default | --additional] [--limit 30] [--page-token]
    │   ├── view <profile-id|default>
    │   ├── create (--default | --name <name>)
    │   │          [--media-encryption <auto|srtp>]
    │   ├── edit <profile-id|default> [--name <name>]
    │   │        [--media-encryption <auto|srtp>]
    │   ├── inbound
    │   │   ├── set <profile-id|default> --sip-uri <uri>
    │   │   │       [--sip-username <username> [--sip-password-file <path>]
    │   │   │        | --clear-sip-credentials] [--yes]
    │   │   ├── edit <profile-id|default> [--sip-uri <uri>]
    │   │   │        [--sip-username <username> [--sip-password-file <path>]
    │   │   │         | --clear-sip-credentials] [--yes]
    │   │   └── clear <profile-id|default> [--yes]
    │   ├── outbound
    │   │   ├── enable <profile-id|default>
    │   │   │          [--digest-algorithm <sha-256|md5>]
    │   │   │          [--idempotency-key <key>]
    │   │   ├── edit <profile-id|default>
    │   │   │        --digest-algorithm <sha-256|md5> [--yes]
    │   │   ├── rotate <profile-id|default> [--mode <normal|emergency>]
    │   │   │          [--idempotency-key <key>] [--yes]
    │   │   └── revoke <profile-id|default> [--yes]
    │   └── delete <profile-id> [--unassign-lines] [--yes]
    └── assignment
        ├── view <resource-id>
        ├── set --profile-id <profile-id> --resource-id <id> ... [--yes]
        └── clear --resource-id <id> ... [--yes]
```

## List inventory

Each list command asks for a currently enabled ability, so one resource can
appear in more than one list — a line that does both iMessage and voice shows
up in both.

```sh
photon project platform list           # the catalog of available platforms
photon project platform sms list --limit 100
photon project platform email list
photon project platform imessage list
photon project platform telegram list
photon project platform voice list --page-token opaque-token
```

SMS, email, iMessage, and Telegram lists also fetch pending provisioning work
and show it in a separate section when there is any. Their JSON is Photon's
page of resources as it sends it, with the operations still setting up a
resource of that kind under `provisioning`, each as Photon sends it:

```json
{"resources":[{"resourceId":"pho_res_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"cosmos_line","state":"active","abilities":["imessage","pstn_voice"],"detail":{"imessageHealth":{"account":{"cause":null,"status":"available"},"imessageOnPhoneNumber":{"cause":null,"status":"available"},"mms":{"cause":null,"status":"available"},"newConversations":{"cause":null,"status":"available"},"outboundPhoneCalling":{"cause":null,"status":"available"},"overallStatus":"available","photonSystemComponents":{"cause":null,"status":"available"},"sms":{"cause":null,"status":"available"}},"phoneNumber":"+14155550125","whatsappHealth":{"overallStatus":"available"}},"createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z"}],"provisioning":[{"operationId":"pho_opr_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"resource.provision","resourceType":"cosmos_line","state":"running","createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","startedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z"}],"nextPageToken":"optional-resource-token"}
```

`nextPageToken` applies to resources only — provisioning operations are
paginated for you.

Voice lists resources only, and its JSON is Photon's response as it sends it:
`{"resources": [...], "nextPageToken": "..."}`.

`platform list` prints Photon's catalog of platforms as the API sends it:

```json
{"platforms":[{"type":"sms","displayName":"SMS","description":"Text messages over a project-owned phone number."}]}
```

## Buy a number

`sms purchase` provisions one US local SMS number. `--country US` is the default;
use `--area-code 415` to request a particular area code. `imessage purchase`
provisions one dedicated iMessage line. Both confirm first unless you pass
`--yes`:

```sh
photon project platform sms purchase \
  --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s \
  --idempotency-key provision-project-123-sms \
  --yes
```

<Warning>
  An idempotency key identifies one logical resource **forever**. Reusing a key
  resumes that same purchase rather than buying a second number. Use a fresh
  key when you want another resource.
</Warning>

The CLI sends the purchase once, then polls the resulting operation until it
succeeds, fails, or is cancelled. iMessage provisioning can take hours, so
there is no client-side timeout. Success prints Photon's last operation read,
with the number or line in `resource`. When that read has no resource yet, the
CLI reads the operation once more and prints that read:

```json
{"operation":{"operationId":"pho_opr_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"resource.provision","resourceType":"voip_line","resourceId":"pho_res_...","state":"succeeded","cancellable":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","startedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","endedAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z"},"resource":{"resourceId":"pho_res_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"voip_line","state":"active","abilities":["sms","pstn_voice"],"detail":{"phoneNumber":"+14155550125"},"createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z"}}
```

<kbd>Ctrl-C</kbd> stops the local wait only — the purchase keeps running on
Photon. The journal keeps the remote operation ID, so
`photon operation replay <local-id>` resumes polling without buying anything
else. See [Recoverable operations](/cli/operations).

## Release an SMS number

`sms delete` reads the resource to check that it still has SMS ability, then
releases it with `DELETE /v1/projects/{id}/platforms/resources/{resourceId}`,
polls the release until it ends, and prints Photon's last operation read:

```sh
photon project platform sms delete pho_res_... --yes --json
```

```json
{"operation":{"operationId":"pho_opr_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"resource.release","resourceType":"voip_line","resourceId":"pho_res_...","state":"succeeded","cancellable":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","startedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","endedAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z"}}
```

When Photon answers the release as already ended, there's nothing to poll, and
the command prints that answer under `operation`, as an operation read holds
it. It's the one place the CLI shapes these documents, so the command keeps
the operation read's `{operation, resource}` shape on every path. The
`--no-wait` answers of `email connect` and `telegram connect` print the same
way.

When [`operation replay`](/cli/operations#replay) gets a 404 from Photon
because the number is gone, or because the release went through and Photon no
longer has its operation, the replay's `result` is
`{"deleted": true, "resourceId": "pho_res_..."}`. When the replay
has no release operation to read because the number was already retired, its
`result` is the number as Photon sends it, `{"resource": {...}}`.

There is no dedicated iMessage or voice release command.

## Cancel provisioning in progress

`platform operation cancel` asks Photon to withdraw an in-progress remote
provision:

```sh
photon project platform operation cancel pho_opr_... \
  --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s \
  --yes \
  --json
```

Pending email-domain, Telegram bot, and dedicated iMessage provisioning can be
cancelled; SMS provider orders cannot. Photon returns `OPERATION_NOT_CANCELLABLE` if the
operation is past the point of no return or finished first. Cancelling here
does not remove the local journal record.

With `--json` it prints the operation as Photon returns it:

```json
{"operationId":"pho_opr_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"resource.provision","resourceType":"cosmos_line","state":"cancelled","cancellable":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","startedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","endedAt":"2026-08-01T18:05:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:05:00.000Z"}
```

## Connect an email domain

`email connect` starts SES and DNS verification. The CLI normalizes case, a
trailing root dot, and internationalized names before sending, and rejects
schemes, paths, ports, wildcards, IP addresses, and malformed labels locally:

```sh
photon project platform email connect bücher.example \
  --idempotency-key connect-project-123-email
```

It submits once, then polls until Photon publishes six DNS records and prints
them straight away. If your DNS provider supports Domain Connect, the CLI also
prints a signed automatic-setup URL and opens it in a browser — pass
`--no-browser` to print it without opening. The manual records are always
printed as a fallback.

The command then keeps waiting until Photon verifies the records, with or
without `--json`. <kbd>Ctrl-C</kbd> or `--no-wait` stops the local wait
without cancelling remote verification.

With `--json`, the records and the waiting line go to stderr, and stdout gets
Photon's last operation read when the command ends,
`{"operation": {...}, "resource": {...}}`, with the records in
`operation.detail.dnsRecords` and the email domain in `resource`. Add
`--no-wait` to get Photon's answer as soon as DNS is prepared: the setup, under
`operation`, with the six records in `operation.detail.dnsRecords` and
`automaticSetup` with `applyUrl`, `dnsProviderId`, and `providerDisplayName`
next to them when Domain Connect is available. When Photon's answer doesn't
hold the records yet, the CLI reads the operation until it does and prints that
read, with the `resource` when the read holds it. With or without
`--json`, the CLI checks the records first and fails with
`PROJECT_PROTOCOL_ERROR` when they're invalid.

Re-read the published instructions at any time with the remote operation ID —
this hits Photon directly and needs neither the original idempotency key nor
the local journal:

```sh
photon project platform email dns pho_opr_...
```

With `--json` it prints Photon's response for the operation,
`{"operation": {...}, "resource": {...}}`. The six records are in
`operation.detail.dnsRecords`, next to `automaticSetup` and `verification`
when Photon has them, and `resource` is the email domain once verification
has finished. With or without `--json`, the CLI checks the records first and
fails with `PROJECT_PROTOCOL_ERROR` when they're invalid.

Disconnect by resource ID. The command reads the resource to check that it's
an email domain, releases it with
`DELETE /v1/projects/{id}/platforms/resources/{resourceId}`, polls the release
until it ends, and prints Photon's last operation read:

```sh
photon project platform email disconnect pho_res_... --yes --json
```

```json
{"operation":{"operationId":"pho_opr_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"resource.release","resourceType":"email_domain","resourceId":"pho_res_...","state":"succeeded","cancellable":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","startedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","endedAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z"}}
```

When [`operation replay`](/cli/operations#replay) gets a 404 from Photon
because the domain is gone, or because the release went through and Photon no
longer has its operation, the replay's `result` is
`{"disconnected": true, "resourceId": "pho_res_..."}`.

## Connect a Telegram bot

A project sends and receives on Telegram through a bot that you own. `telegram
connect` starts a setup and hands it over to Telegram, where you choose one of
your bots or create a new one and confirm the project:

```sh
photon project platform telegram connect
```

The command prints a setup link, shows it as a QR code when it runs in a
terminal, and opens it in a browser — pass `--no-browser` to print it without
opening. Open the link on any device that has Telegram; the link stays usable
until the setup finishes.

`connect` always adds a bot. If the project already has one, the command says
so and asks before it starts a setup. Without a terminal, pass `--yes`:
without it the command exits `2` with `CONFIRMATION_REQUIRED` and starts
nothing. Use `telegram list` to see the bots a project has.

The command then waits until the bot is connected and prints it, with or
without `--json`. <kbd>Ctrl-C</kbd> or `--no-wait` stops the local wait
without cancelling the setup. With `--json`, the link and the waiting line go
to stderr and stdout gets Photon's last operation read when the command ends,
`{"operation": {...}, "resource": {...}}`, with the bot in `resource`.

With `--no-wait --json`, the command returns as soon as the setup is accepted,
so automation gets the setup to act on, under `operation`, with the link in
`operation.detail.setupUrl`: Photon's answer to the request that started it,
or Photon's read of the setup when the command continues one already in
progress. It still opens the link unless you pass `--no-browser`:

```json
{"operation":{"operationId":"pho_opr_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"resource.provision","resourceType":"telegram_bot","state":"pending","cancellable":true,"detail":{"setupUrl":"https://t.me/...?start=..."},"createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z"}}
```

The JSON holds the link as Photon sends it. Text shows the link, and the
command opens it, only after the CLI checks that it's a Telegram setup link.

<Warning>
  Treat `setupUrl` as a secret. Whoever opens it can connect a bot to the
  project, so keep it out of logs and shared channels.
</Warning>

A project has one Telegram setup at a time. If one is already in progress,
`telegram connect` continues it — it prints the same link again and says
`Continuing the Telegram bot setup already in progress.` — so running the
command again is also how you pick a setup back up. The JSON has no `resumed`
field: no Photon response carries it, and the rule is to print the response as
sent. `kubectl apply -o json` does the same: it prints the object, and
`created` or `configured` appears only in text. To start over, cancel the
setup and connect again:

```sh
photon project platform operation cancel pho_opr_... --yes
photon project platform telegram connect
```

Inspect a connected bot by resource ID. With `--json` it prints the resource
as Photon sends it. `detail` holds its name, username, and public link, plus
`connectionIssue` when the connection needs attention — for example after the
bot changed owner in Telegram:

```sh
photon project platform telegram view pho_res_... --json
```

```json
{"resourceId":"pho_res_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"telegram_bot","state":"active","abilities":["telegram"],"detail":{"displayName":"Alex Support","username":"AlexSupportBot","url":"https://t.me/AlexSupportBot"},"createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z"}
```

Disconnect by resource ID. The bot stops sending and receiving through Photon;
it is not deleted from Telegram and stays yours. The command reads the resource
to check that it's a Telegram bot, releases it with
`DELETE /v1/projects/{id}/platforms/resources/{resourceId}`, polls the release
until it ends, and prints Photon's last operation read:

```sh
photon project platform telegram disconnect pho_res_... --yes --json
```

```json
{"operation":{"operationId":"pho_opr_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"resource.release","resourceType":"telegram_bot","resourceId":"pho_res_...","state":"succeeded","cancellable":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","startedAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","endedAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-01T18:01:00.000Z"}}
```

When [`operation replay`](/cli/operations#replay) gets a 404 from Photon
because the bot is gone, or because the release went through and Photon no
longer has its operation, the replay's `result` is
`{"disconnected": true, "resourceId": "pho_res_..."}`.

## iMessage shared-line assignments

A dedicated iMessage line can be shared. An **assignment** binds one of your
users to the line so their messages are attributed to them:

```sh
photon project platform imessage assignment create \
  --phone-number +14155550125 \
  --email ada@example.com \
  --first-name Ada \
  --last-name Lovelace \
  --idempotency-key assign-ada

photon project platform imessage assignment list
photon project platform imessage assignment view pho_asg_...
```

`--phone-number` is required and must be E.164. The name and email fields are
optional and only affect how the user is presented.

`assignment list` shows active assignments. Add `--include-released` to see
released ones too:

```sh
photon project platform imessage assignment list --include-released --limit 100
```

Releasing frees the user's slot on the line and asks for confirmation first:

```sh
photon project platform imessage assignment release pho_asg_... --yes --json
```

With `--json`, each command prints Photon's response as the API sends it.
`assignment list` prints the API's envelope,
`{"assignments": [...], "nextPageToken": "..."}`, with `nextPageToken` only
when more assignments remain. `create`, `view` and `release` print the
assignment itself. Its `handle` is the user's phone number, and after
`release` its `releasedAt` is set:

```json
{"assignmentId":"pho_asg_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","platform":"imessage","handle":"+14155550125","assignedPhoneNumber":"+14155550126","email":"ada@example.com","firstName":"Ada","lastName":"Lovelace","createdAt":"2026-08-01T18:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-02T18:00:00.000Z","releasedAt":"2026-08-02T18:00:00.000Z"}
```

When [`operation replay`](/cli/operations#replay) finds that a release
already went through, Photon answers 404 and sends no assignment, so the
replay's `result` is `{"assignmentId": "pho_asg_...", "released": true}`.

`imessage view` shows whether the project's iMessage runs on a shared or a
dedicated line. With `--json` it prints Photon's settings, such as
`{"mode": "shared", "maxUserAssignments": 10, "dedicatedLineAvailableNow": true}`.

## WhatsApp Business

Each project has one connected WhatsApp Business account. The `account list`
command returns zero or one account and includes its capacity, review status,
restrictions, and alerts. Account pagination is no longer supported.

List the connected account and active senders:

```sh
photon project platform whatsapp-business account list
photon project platform whatsapp-business number list
photon project platform whatsapp-business number view pho_res_...
```

With `--json`, `account list` prints the account as Photon sends it, in a
list: `{"accounts": [...]}`. Photon answers with the project's one account,
or with an error when none is connected, and then the list is empty,
`{"accounts": []}`, and the command exits `0` with `--json` or without
it, as `gh pr list --json` prints `[]` and `kubectl get -o json` prints a
`List` with empty `items`.

`number list` lists active senders only: it asks Photon for them, and its JSON
is the page Photon sends, so it lists the same senders as the text:

```json
{"resources":[{"resourceId":"pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2t","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"whatsapp_sender","state":"active","abilities":["whatsapp_business"],"detail":{"account":{"wabaId":"123456789012345"},"phoneNumber":"+14155550199","phoneNumberId":"987654321098765","qualityRating":"GREEN","qualityRatingObservedAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:30.000Z","displayName":"Acme Support","displayNameStatus":"APPROVED","voipResourceId":"pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s"},"createdAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-26T20:01:00.000Z"}],"nextPageToken":"optional-resource-token"}
```

`--limit` counts the senders Photon returns before the CLI leaves out the
ones that aren't active, so a page can list fewer senders than `--limit`.

### Human onboarding wizard

Run `number add` without `--source` in an interactive terminal. The wizard
first asks for one simple choice: **Bring Your Own Number** or **Use a Photon
SMS Number**.

- Bring Your Own Number opens focused dashboard onboarding and stops. Finish
  Meta Embedded Signup and verification in the browser; the CLI does not wait
  or refresh inventory.
- Use a Photon SMS Number lets you select an eligible existing number or buy a
  new US local SMS number. Purchased numbers remain in the project if you stop
  onboarding later.
- The wizard always asks which connected WABA to use, even when there is only
  one. Selecting a connected WABA provisions directly. Selecting **Connect
  another WABA with Meta** opens Embedded Signup while the terminal retrieves
  and displays the SMS OTP.

```sh
photon project platform whatsapp-business number add \
  --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s
```

### Automation and agent flows

Automation must pass `--source`; `--json` without it fails instead of opening
the human wizard.

Bring-your-own-number onboarding is a browser handoff:

```sh
photon project platform whatsapp-business number add \
  --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s \
  --source byon \
  --json
```

The command attempts to open the dashboard and prints a guide for the person
who finishes setup: the dashboard URL and the numbered steps. Without `--json`
the guide is the command's output, on stdout. With `--json` the guide goes to
stderr as text, and stdout holds one `handoff` object with the dashboard URL,
which an agent can pass to that person. No Photon response describes a browser
handoff, so `handoff` is the CLI's own document. Embedded Signup requires human browser
interaction, and the dashboard owns verification and final connection. Use
`--no-browser` when an agent cannot launch a browser; the same guide is
printed, and the object has `browserAttempted: false`.

```json
{"handoff":{"kind":"whatsapp-embedded-signup","source":"byon","requiresHumanInteraction":true,"browserAttempted":true,"opened":true,"projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","url":"https://app.beta.photon.codes/pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s/platforms/whatsapp-business?onboarding=cli&source=byon"}}
```

Connect an eligible Photon SMS number directly to an already-connected WABA:

```sh
photon project platform whatsapp-business number add \
  --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s \
  --source photon \
  --waba-id 123456789012345 \
  --voip-resource-id pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s \
  --display-name "Acme Support" \
  --idempotency-key customer-123-photon-wa-1 \
  --json
```

Photon owns the SMS-code request, verification, and Meta registration for this
direct API path. The CLI records the accepted operation and polls it to a
terminal result, so interrupted work can be resumed with `photon operation
replay <local-operation-id>`. Success prints Photon's last operation read,
with the sender in `resource`, as `sms purchase` does:

```json
{"operation":{"operationId":"pho_opr_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"resource.provision","resourceType":"whatsapp_sender","resourceId":"pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2t","state":"succeeded","cancellable":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:00.000Z","startedAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:00.000Z","endedAt":"2026-08-26T20:01:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-26T20:01:00.000Z"},"resource":{"resourceId":"pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2t","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"whatsapp_sender","state":"active","abilities":["whatsapp_business"],"detail":{"account":{"wabaId":"123456789012345"},"phoneNumber":"+14155550199","phoneNumberId":"987654321098765","qualityRating":"GREEN","qualityRatingObservedAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:30.000Z","displayName":"Acme Support","displayNameStatus":"APPROVED","voipResourceId":"pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s"},"createdAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-26T20:01:00.000Z"}}
```

To use that Photon number with a new or different WABA, hand it to the
dashboard instead:

```sh
photon project platform whatsapp-business number add \
  --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s \
  --source photon \
  --new-waba \
  --voip-resource-id pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s \
  --json
```

This command validates that the number is active, SMS-capable, belongs to the
project, and is not already linked. It then opens the dashboard and exits. The
dashboard owns both Embedded Signup and OTP display; use the dashboard or rerun
the interactive CLI if you need guided OTP handling. No provisioning mutation
or local operation record is created by this handoff. The `handoff` object
holds the SMS number as Photon sends it in `resource`, as the CLI's own
documents hold API objects whole (`billing overview`, `link` and
`payment-method setup` do too):

```json
{"handoff":{"kind":"whatsapp-embedded-signup","source":"photon","requiresHumanInteraction":true,"browserAttempted":true,"opened":true,"projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","resource":{"resourceId":"pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"voip_line","state":"active","abilities":["sms","pstn_voice"],"detail":{"phoneNumber":"+14155550199"},"createdAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:00.000Z"},"url":"https://app.beta.photon.codes/pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s/platforms/whatsapp-business?onboarding=cli&source=photon&voipResourceId=pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s&verification=dashboard","verificationOwner":"dashboard"}}
```

`--new-waba` and `--waba-id` are mutually exclusive. Photon source always
requires `--voip-resource-id`. BYON rejects WABA, Photon-resource,
display-name, and idempotency flags. `--no-browser` applies only to browser
handoffs.

### WhatsApp sender JSON

`number view` prints the sender as Photon sends it, a retired one too. Text
shows `phoneNumber` in E.164 form, and JSON as Photon sends it:

```json
{"resourceId":"pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2t","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"whatsapp_sender","state":"active","abilities":["whatsapp_business"],"detail":{"account":{"wabaId":"123456789012345"},"phoneNumber":"+14155550199","phoneNumberId":"987654321098765","qualityRating":"GREEN","qualityRatingObservedAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:30.000Z","displayName":"Acme Support","displayNameStatus":"APPROVED","voipResourceId":"pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s"},"createdAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-26T20:01:00.000Z"}
```

`number list` displays phone number, display name, name status, quality,
Photon source, and resource ID. Deletion confirms against
`phoneNumber`:

```sh
photon project platform whatsapp-business number delete pho_res_... --yes --json
```

`number delete` reads the sender to check that it's an active WhatsApp
Business sender, then releases it with
`DELETE /v1/projects/{id}/platforms/resources/{resourceId}`, polls the release
until it ends, and prints Photon's last operation read, with `operation.detail`
as Photon sends it:

```json
{"operation":{"operationId":"pho_opr_...","projectId":"pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s","type":"resource.release","resourceType":"whatsapp_sender","resourceId":"pho_res_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2t","state":"succeeded","cancellable":false,"createdAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:00.000Z","startedAt":"2026-08-26T20:00:00.000Z","endedAt":"2026-08-26T20:01:00.000Z","updatedAt":"2026-08-26T20:01:00.000Z"}}
```

When Photon answers the release as already ended, the command prints that
answer under `operation`, as [`sms delete`](#release-an-sms-number) does.
When [`operation replay`](/cli/operations#replay) gets a 404 from Photon
because the sender is gone, or because the release went through and Photon no
longer has its operation, the replay's `result` is
`{"deleted": true, "resourceId": "pho_res_..."}`. When the replay has no
release operation to read because the sender was already retired, its
`result` is the sender as Photon sends it, `{"resource": {...}}`.

## Voice routing profiles

Voice inventory and voice routing answer different questions. `voice list`
shows resources with an active `pstn_voice` ability. A **profile** describes
where Photon should send an inbound call once that check passes.

```sh
photon project platform voice profile create \
  --default \
  --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s

photon project platform voice profile inbound set default \
  --sip-uri 'sips:agent@customer.example:5061' \
  --yes \
  --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s

photon project platform voice profile view default --project pho_prj_01k9yq3m7d2c8x4v6b5n0p1r2s
```

A project has one default profile and any number of additional profiles. Use
the literal `default` with `profile view`, `profile edit`, or any inbound or
outbound command.
The CLI uses `/profiles/default` to read that profile, then sends directional
changes through `/profiles/{profileId}` with the stable ID returned by the
read. The default cannot be renamed, deleted, or reassigned, and it must exist
before you can create an additional profile.

A line with no override follows the project default. Assigning an additional
profile creates an override; clearing it returns the line to the current
default. `profile edit` can rename an additional profile and change media
encryption on either an additional or the default profile. Inbound and
outbound delivery have dedicated commands beneath the profile. Editing requires
at least one of `--name` or `--media-encryption`; the default cannot be renamed.

```sh
photon project platform voice profile edit default --media-encryption srtp
```

### SIP routes

A profile can carry one inbound SIP destination. The scheme picks the
transport: bare `sip:` normalizes to UDP, `;transport=tcp` selects TCP,
`;transport=udp` selects UDP, and `sips:` selects TLS. Photon owns the
authoritative SIP syntax and public-host validation, so there are no separate
transport options. Use `--media-encryption auto` or `--media-encryption srtp`
on profile creation or editing; `srtp` requires encrypted media.

Default creation needs only `--default`; Photon assigns the immutable name
`Default Profile`. Additional profile creation requires `--name`. Both create
direction-neutral profiles, so configure delivery afterward. Use
`inbound set` to create or fully replace the destination, `inbound edit` to
change selected fields, and `inbound clear` to disable inbound delivery:

```sh
photon project platform voice profile inbound set default \
  --sip-uri 'sips:agent@customer.example:5061' \
  --yes

photon project platform voice profile inbound edit pho_vpf_... \
  --sip-uri 'sip:agent@customer.example:5061;transport=tcp' \
  --yes

photon project platform voice profile inbound clear pho_vpf_... --yes
```

Every command reads the current profile first and sends its latest version.
Route changes on a profile with assigned lines require confirmation because
they take effect for those lines immediately.

### Endpoint credentials

Credentials are replaced as an atomic username and password pair. **Passwords
are never accepted as command-line options.** With `--sip-username` and no
`--sip-password-file`, an interactive terminal prompts with hidden input.
Without a terminal, pass `--sip-password-file`, or `--sip-password-file -` to
read the exact password from stdin.

A password file is read exactly as written — spaces and trailing newlines are
significant — so create it deliberately:

```sh
printf %s 'private-password' > /secure/path/sip-password
photon project platform voice profile inbound edit pho_vpf_... \
  --sip-username agent-user \
  --sip-password-file /secure/path/sip-password \
  --yes
```

Photon's responses carry the username, not the password.
Passwords, SIP destinations, and profile bodies are redacted from `--debug`
output and never written to the local journal. Omitting credential flags
preserves the existing write-only secret, `--clear-sip-credentials` clears both
fields, and `--sip-username` replaces the username and password together. The
CLI never sends a masked password from a previous response back to Photon.

### Outbound credentials

Outbound calling supports credential lifecycle commands and password-preserving
authentication edits:

```sh
photon project platform voice profile outbound enable default \
  --digest-algorithm sha-256 \
  --idempotency-key enable-default-outbound-1

photon project platform voice profile outbound edit default \
  --digest-algorithm md5

photon project platform voice profile outbound rotate default \
  --mode normal \
  --idempotency-key rotate-default-outbound-1 \
  --yes

photon project platform voice profile outbound revoke default --yes
```

`outbound enable` creates the first credential for a profile with no active
outbound credential. It defaults to SHA-256 and always sends that explicit
choice to Photon. Select `--digest-algorithm md5` only for weaker legacy
compatibility. Photon supports MD5 over the UDP, TCP, and TLS transports
returned in the outbound endpoint list. Enable output reports the active
algorithm and each endpoint's transport, host, and port so the SIP client can
use the connection guidance returned by Photon.

`outbound edit` changes the active Digest algorithm while preserving the
username and password, including existing validity and rotation grace metadata.
Use it to switch algorithms on an enabled profile. MD5 is the weaker legacy
option; select it only for compatibility. Selecting SHA-256 can stop a PBX that
does not support it from authenticating. The command asks for confirmation;
use `--yes` to skip the prompt in automation.

The CLI resolves `default` to its stable profile ID and sends the current
profile's top-level `version` as `expectedVersion`. Even a same-value edit goes
to Photon so the server can validate that version and return its authoritative
result. A stale version produces `VOICE_PROFILE_VERSION_CONFLICT`: read the
current profile and retry. `VOICE_OUTBOUND_OPERATION_PENDING` instead means
credential setup or rotation must finish first. Resume the original enable or
rotate request with its original idempotency key, or replay its recovery
record; after it completes, read the current profile and retry the edit.

Edit output contains the returned `outbound` connection and authentication
metadata and `profileVersion`, with no password or display-once secret warning.
Algorithm edits require no idempotency key, operation journal entry, or secret
acknowledgement.

`outbound rotate` replaces an existing credential while preserving the
active Digest algorithm, so rotation intentionally has no algorithm flag;
`normal` keeps the previous credential valid for one hour, while `emergency`
removes that grace period. `outbound revoke` disables outbound calling. It does
not delete the profile, inbound route, or line assignments.

Enable and rotation output `password` at most once. Save it from stdout
immediately: the CLI never persists it or writes it to debug output. Before the
request, the CLI privately journals the exact profile reference, selected
enablement digest algorithm, rotation mode, and `expectedVersion`. If the
result is unresolved, rerunning the command with the same supplied idempotency
key or using `operation replay` resends that exact body without rereading the
now-mutated profile. An idempotent replay returns
`password: null`; the mutation succeeded, but the display-once password cannot
be reproduced.

After successful output is flushed, the recovery record is removed. Do not
rerun an already-confirmed success with its old key; deliberately rotate with
the returned `profileVersion` and a new idempotency key to issue another
password. An omitted idempotency key is generated for the current invocation.
A supplied key must identify only that same unresolved enable or rotation
action. The CLI also retries one transport interruption within an invocation
with the same key and unchanged request body.

### Assignments

```sh
photon project platform voice assignment set \
  --profile-id pho_vpf_... \
  --resource-id pho_res_first... \
  --resource-id pho_res_second... \
  --yes

photon project platform voice assignment clear \
  --resource-id pho_res_first... \
  --yes
```

The CLI reads each current assignment before changing it and sends its version,
so a concurrent change surfaces as a conflict rather than a silent overwrite —
view the assignment and repeat the command. One changed line uses the
single-line endpoint; 2 to 100 use the atomic batch endpoint. Duplicate IDs and
batches over 100 are rejected locally, and already-correct lines are no-ops.
Assignment output shows `default` when there is no override.

Each change goes to the `--resource-id` given. If Photon answers the read of a
line with another line's assignment, the command fails with
`VOICE_PROTOCOL_ERROR` and changes nothing. If Photon's answer to a change
leaves out a line it changed, the command fails with `VOICE_PROTOCOL_ERROR` and
names the line, because the change may have been made: check it with
`assignment view`.

Inbound changes on a profile with assigned lines, outbound algorithm edits,
rotation or revocation, assignment changes, and profile deletion all require
confirmation.
`--yes` skips only that prompt. Deleting an assigned profile additionally
needs `--unassign-lines`, which asks Photon to atomically clear the overrides,
return those lines to the default, and delete the profile.

### JSON envelopes

Every Voice command except `profile delete`, `assignment set` and
`assignment clear` prints Photon's response as it sends it, and a field Photon
adds shows up in the JSON. The commands that read the profile for its version
first print the response to the change. Photon answers `profile delete` with no
body, so it names the profile it deleted. `assignment set` and `clear` change
one line or several, so they list one assignment for each `--resource-id`, in
that order. For a line the command changed, it's the assignment Photon returned
for the change, or, after a `clear` that changes one line, which Photon answers
with no body, the line as read after the change. A line that already uses the
profile (for `clear`, one that's already unassigned) isn't changed, and shows
as read before the change.

| Command | JSON |
| --- | --- |
| `profile create`, `view`, `edit` | The profile: `{"profileId": "...", "name": "...", "configuration": {...}, "version": 2, ...}` |
| `profile list` | `{"profiles": [...], "nextPageToken": "..."}` |
| `profile inbound set`, `edit`, `clear` | `{"inbound": ... or null, "profileVersion": 2}` |
| `profile outbound enable` | `{"outbound": {...}, "password": "..." or null, "profileVersion": 3}` |
| `profile outbound edit` | `{"outbound": {...}, "profileVersion": 4}` |
| `profile outbound rotate` | `{"password": "..." or null, "previousPasswordValidUntil": "..." or null, "profileVersion": 4}` |
| `profile outbound revoke` | `{"outbound": null, "profileVersion": 5}` |
| `profile delete` | `{"deleted": true, "profileId": "...", "returnedToDefaultLineCount": 0}` |
| `assignment view` | The assignment: `{"resourceId": "...", "profileId": "...", "resourceVersion": 3, ...}` |
| `assignment set`, `clear` | `{"assignments": [...]}` |

`profile list` accepts mutually exclusive `--default` and `--additional`
filters.

<Note>
  The active `pstn_voice` ability remains the source of truth for admission.
  Saving or assigning a profile does not by itself grant voice access.
</Note>
